  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "French bal-folk folk-dance-revival movement emerging in 1970s post-1968 French counter-cultural folk-music revival, reconstructing the regional folk-dance repertoires (bourrée Auvergnate, mazurka, scottish, polka, valse Aveyronaise) for participatory ballroom-style social-dance contexts. Performance: small ensemble of hurdy-gurdy, cabrette or veuze bagpipe, vielle à roue, fiddle, accordion, acoustic guitar, plus voice for sung-dance pieces. Distinguished from earlier French bal-musette by bal-folk's regional-folk-revival (vs Parisian-urban-cabaret) functional context. The Bal-Folk association network and the annual Saint-Chartier (later renamed Le Son Continu) festival anchor institutional continuity.",
